{
    "title": "Continuation of the National Emergency With Respect to Serious Human Rights Abuse and Corruption",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 12, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-12/pdf/2025-22785.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has extended for one more year the national emergency first declared in 2017 to address serious human rights abuses and corruption around the world. The administration says these problems, originating largely outside the United States, still pose an unusual and extraordinary threat to U.S. national security, foreign policy, and the economy, so the emergency and its related authorities will stay in place past December 20, 2025.\n\nIn practice, this keeps intact the government\u2019s ability to freeze assets, block transactions, and bar entry to people and organizations it determines are involved in severe abuses or significant corruption. Supporters say this benefits victims, human rights defenders, and anti\u2011corruption groups by maintaining pressure on abusers and by signaling that access to the U.S. financial system is not available to those who engage in wrongdoing. It also gives U.S. diplomats and law enforcement continued tools to respond quickly when new cases arise, and it can level the playing field for companies that want to compete without paying bribes.\n\nThose most negatively affected are the individuals and networks that are designated under these authorities, as they can lose access to funds and markets. There can also be ripple effects for banks and businesses abroad that are connected to them, and for legitimate firms that must spend more time and resources on screening partners and transactions. Some humanitarian efforts may face delays when organizations over\u2011comply out of caution. Critics also worry about the long-running nature of emergency powers and the risk of mistakes or a lack of transparency in how designations are made.\n\nThe notice will be published in the Federal Register and sent to Congress. For now, the emergency remains in force for another year, with the option to renew again if future conditions warrant it.",
